concept :the_halving_count do
  name "The Halving Count"
  aka "半衰计", "counting halves"
  tags :medicine, :dependency, :legibility
  prominence :forgotten

  prose <<~PROSE
    Nothing measured on a body means what it says until whatever was in the body beforehand has halved five times, which leaves about a thirtieth of it. Symbiosis clinics count in 半衰, halvings. The benches that make substitute compounds in the #{ref :the_dead_counties, "Dead Counties"} count in halves. Both count from the last dose or from the cut, and neither counts from the hour the person was admitted.

    Neither tradition arrived at the figure. Both took it from a pharmacology older than either government, which fixed five halvings as the interval after which a second reading may be compared with a first. The count belongs to the compound rather than to the room: something that halves in six hours clears inside a day and a half, and something that halves in nine days does not clear inside a seven-day #{ref :separation_leave, "leave"} at all.
  PROSE

  prose <<~PROSE, section: :mechanics, heading: "What The Count Rules Out"
    At #{ref :mulberry_court_clinic, "Mulberry Court Clinic"} the completion card's first section is ruled off with a diagonal. Temperature, sleep, inflammation and strip colour are entered there for the first two days of a leave and are not compared with the sixth day's figures. A garment's regulation persists in blood and lymph after the cut, and most adults arrive on a continuous course of something. The intake card carries one line for the date of the last dose of anything, filled in before the garment is cut.

    #{ref :ghost_pharmacology, "Ghost pharmacology"} meets the count from the other side. A substitute must hold the signature of the drug it replaces across the whole of that drug's count, because a guardian compares what it reads now against what it holds from before. A compound that matches on the first day and steps on the third produces a reading a guardian classifies as a missed dose, and a missed dose brings a review, a visit and an adjusted regimen. #{ref :cabin_creek_bench, "The Cabin Creek bench"} exists to find the step.
  PROSE

  prose <<~PROSE, section: :limits, heading: "Carry"
    A carry is a reading produced by the period before an interruption and entered against the interruption itself. A fever on the third day of a leave, attributed to the cut. A substitute rejected for a step that belonged to the compound it was replacing. Both traditions treat a carry as the characteristic error of their own work rather than as an unlucky result.

    Neither governing system keeps the count. The municipal health record dates a leave from the cut, so the halvings exist on a steward's card and nowhere in the neighbourhood's record; a coalition guardian holds a continuous series with no boundary in it at all. #{ref :the_second_room_fever, "The Second-Room Fever"} is what a committee concluded from municipal figures with no count under them.
  PROSE

  log "2026-08-09 — Pharmacokinetics decides the unit and the failure. A washout interval in a crossover study is specified in half-lives rather than in days, with five taken as the conventional figure because it leaves about three per cent of the starting quantity, and carryover — an effect from the previous period entered against the next — is the named failure when the interval is short. Borrowed: counting in halvings from the last dose, and the name for the error. Reason: the interval an interruption actually costs is set by a body's own clearance rate, so a calendar cannot state it and two traditions with no contact had to count the same way."
end
